1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,450
Chapter 23: Abnormal Labor.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:30,687
Dystocia Abnormalities Of The Expulsive Forces Prematurely Ruptured Membranes At Term Precipitous Labor And Delivery Fetopelvic Disproportion Face Presentation Brow Presentation Transverse Lie Compound Presentation Complications With Dystocia References Labor arrest, abnormal fetal presentation, or fetal jeopardy are indications for a large percentage of primary cesarean deliveries in the United States .

4
00:00:30,687 --> 00:00:37,775
Lowering dystocia rates offers the potential to decrease rates of this surgery and associated maternal morbidity.

5
00:00:37,775 --> 00:00:39,437
Dystocia.

6
00:00:39,437 --> 00:00:45,850
Dystocia literally means difficult labor and is characterized by abnormally slow labor progress.

7
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:49,500
Causes are grouped into three distinct categories.

8
00:00:49,500 --> 00:01:03,525
Mechanistically, these simplify into abnormalities of the powers—poor uterine contractility and maternal expulsive effort; of the passenger—the fetus; and of the passage—the pelvis and lower reproductive tract.

9
00:01:03,525 --> 00:01:08,725
These three groups act singly or in combination to produce dysfunctional labor .

10
00:01:08,725 --> 00:01:17,362
For the powers, uterine contractions may be insufficiently strong or inappropriately coordinated to efface and dilate the cervix.

11
00:01:17,362 --> 00:01:20,125
This is termed uterine dysfunction.

12
00:01:20,125 --> 00:01:26,250
Moreover, during second-stage labor, voluntary maternal pushing may be inadequate.

13
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:33,337
For the passenger, fetal abnormalities of presentation, position, or anatomy may slow progress.

14
00:01:33,337 --> 00:01:39,187
Last, for the passage, structural changes can contract the maternal bony pelvis.

15
00:01:39,187 --> 00:01:45,037
Or, soft tissue abnormalities of the reproductive tract may block fetal descent.

16
00:01:45,037 --> 00:01:47,525
Table 23-1.

17
00:01:47,525 --> 00:01:59,262
Some Causes of Dystocia in Term Vertex Singletons To describe ineffective labors, two commonly used terms are cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD) and failure to progress.

18
00:01:59,262 --> 00:02:06,375
CPD describes obstructed labor resulting from disparity between the fetal head size and maternal pelvis.

19
00:02:06,375 --> 00:02:14,300
The term CPD originated at a time when the main indication for cesarean delivery was overt pelvic contracture from rickets .

20
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:22,987
Such absolute disproportion is now rare, and most cases result from malposition of the fetal head within the pelvis (asynclitism).

21
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:38,637
True disproportion is a tenuous diagnosis because 50 to 75 percent of women undergoing cesarean delivery for this reason subsequently deliver even larger newborns vaginally (Lewkowitz, 2015; Place, 2019).

22
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:48,225
A second phrase, failure to progress in either spontaneous or stimulated labor, has become an increasingly popular description of ineffectual labor.

23
00:02:48,225 --> 00:02:53,637
This term reflects lack of progressive cervical dilation or halted fetal descent.

24
00:02:53,637 --> 00:02:56,862
Abnormalities Of The Expulsive Forces.

25
00:02:56,862 --> 00:02:59,887
# Types of Uterine Dysfunction.

26
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:05,087
Uterine contractions are needed to dilate the cervix and to expel the fetus.

27
00:03:05,087 --> 00:03:11,562
A contraction is initiated by spontaneous action potentials in the membrane of smooth muscle cells.

28
00:03:11,562 --> 00:03:16,375
Unlike the heart, a single pacemaker or its site remain unresolved .

29
00:03:16,375 --> 00:03:23,312
Resulting uterine contractions in normal labor show a rising and falling gradient of myometrial activity .

30
00:03:23,312 --> 00:03:29,837
Normal spontaneous contractions can exert pressures approximating 60 mm Hg .

31
00:03:29,837 --> 00:03:37,300
Even so, the lower limit of contraction pressure required to dilate the cervix is 15 mm Hg .

32
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:43,125
In abnormal labor, two physiological types of uterine dysfunction may develop.

33
00:03:43,125 --> 00:03:51,975
In the more common hypotonic uterine dysfunction, basal tone is normal and uterine contractions have a normal gradient pattern (synchronous).

34
00:03:51,975 --> 00:03:57,400
However, pressure during a contraction is insufficient to dilate the cervix.

35
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:07,787
In the second type, hypertonic uterine dysfunction or incoordinate uterine dysfunction, either basal tone is elevated appreciably or the pressure gradient is distorted.

36
00:04:07,787 --> 00:04:11,287
# Risk Factors for Uterine Dysfunction.

37
00:04:11,287 --> 00:04:15,325
Various factors are implicated in uterine dysfunction.

38
00:04:15,325 --> 00:04:22,875
First, neuraxial analgesia can slow labor and has been associated with longer first and second stages of labor .

39
00:04:22,875 --> 00:04:32,075
With current anesthesia methods, however, its effect on labor length is blunted (Anim-Somuah, 2018; Myers, 2020).

40
00:04:32,075 --> 00:04:41,100
Moreover, cesarean delivery rates are not higher, and supporting studies are presented in Chapter 25 (p. 477).

41
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:45,162
Chorioamnionitis is associated with prolonged labor.

42
00:04:45,162 --> 00:04:47,937
Uterine infection may directly

43
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:58,987
contribute to uterine dysfunction or instead may simply be an associated consequence of prolonged, dysfunctional labor (Mark, 2000; Satin, 1992).

44
00:04:58,937 --> 00:05:05,775
Affected gravidas are monitored for labor progress, and augmentation of protracted labor is prudent .

45
00:05:05,775 --> 00:05:17,325
A higher station at the onset of labor is significantly linked with subsequent dystocia (Friedman, 1965, 1976; Roshanfekr, 1999).

46
00:05:17,325 --> 00:05:25,275
Although a risk factor, most nulliparas without fetal head engagement at diagnosis of active labor still deliver vaginally.

47
00:05:25,275 --> 00:05:31,825
These observations apply especially for parous women because the head typically descends later in labor.

48
00:05:31,825 --> 00:05:43,562
Dystocia rate rises proportionally with maternal age even after adjusting for maternal and fetal weight and parity (Timofeev, 2013; Waldenström, 2017).

49
00:05:43,562 --> 00:05:58,812
Maternal obesity lengthens the first stages of labor by 30 to 60 minutes in nulliparas, even after adjusting for associated diabetes, fetal weight, and parity (Carlhäll, 2013; Kominiarek, 2011).

50
00:05:58,812 --> 00:06:07,575
Dystocia-associated cesarean delivery rates are higher in this group (Fyfe, 2011; Kawakita, 2016).

51
00:06:07,575 --> 00:06:18,125
Growing evidence suggests a pathologic biological effect of obesity on normal parturition (Azais, 2017; Carlson, 2015).

52
00:06:18,125 --> 00:06:20,650
# Labor Disorders.

53
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:26,625
Latent-phase Prolongation Uterine dysfunction can in turn lead to labor abnormalities .

54
00:06:26,625 --> 00:06:39,200
First, the latent phase may be prolonged, which is defined as >20 hours in the nullipara and >14 hours in the multipara (Friedman, 1961, 1963b).

55
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:43,862
In some, uterine contractions cease, suggesting false labor.

56
00:06:43,862 --> 00:06:52,050
In the remainder, an abnormally long latent phase persists and is often treated with amniotomy and oxytocin stimulation .

57
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:58,612
The diagnosis of uterine dysfunction in the latent phase is difficult and commonly is made retrospectively.

58
00:06:58,612 --> 00:07:05,000
Women who are not yet in active labor often are erroneously treated for perceived uterine dysfunction.

59
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,525
Table 23-2.

60
00:07:07,525 --> 00:07:22,525
Abnormal Labor Patterns, Diagnostic Criteria, and Methods of Treatment Active-phase Disorders In active labor, disorders are divided into those with slow progress—a protraction disorder or those with halted progress—an arrest disorder.

61
00:07:22,525 --> 00:07:29,175
Terms presented in Table 23-2 and their diagnostic criteria describe abnormal labor.

62
00:07:29,175 --> 00:07:34,337
To be diagnosed with either of these, a woman must be in the active phase of labor.

63
00:07:34,337 --> 00:07:53,150
The criteria and management of abnormal labor have undergone modification, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2019b) describe these in their Obstetric Care Consensus titled Safe Prevention of the Primary Cesarean Delivery.

64
00:07:53,150 --> 00:08:03,662
The Obstetric Care Consensus was a response to the rising cesarean delivery rate because nearly one third of women who give birth each year in the United States undergoes this surgery .

65
00:08:03,662 --> 00:08:09,700
New recommendations stem from data of normal labor progress in a more contemporary cohort.

66
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:16,962
The World Health Organization (2018) has also revised its recommendations on labor progress.

67
00:08:16,962 --> 00:08:24,400
These guidelines have prompted controversy by their significant revision of the preexisting understanding of abnormal labor.

68
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:26,775
Active-phase Protraction.

69
00:08:26,775 --> 00:08:31,875
Of active-phase disorders, protraction disorders are less well described.

70
00:08:31,875 --> 00:08:40,062
Previously, protraction has been defined as <1 cm/hr cervical dilation for a minimum of 4 hours.

71
00:08:40,062 --> 00:08:47,800
These criteria were adapted from those of Cohen and Friedman (1983) and shown in Table 23-2.

72
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:53,050
For this disorder, observation for further progress is appropriate treatment.

73
00:08:53,050 --> 00:09:05,062
In monitoring active labor, if hypotonic contractions are strongly suspected, internal monitors may be placed with amniotomy and again cervical change and contraction pattern are reassessed.

74
00:09:05,062 --> 00:09:11,900
Deficient Montevideo units and poor active labor progress typically prompts oxytocin augmentation.

75
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:21,112
In accord with the Consensus Committee (2019b), slow but progressive first-stage labor should not be an indication for cesarean delivery.

76
00:09:21,112 --> 00:09:23,312
Active-phase Arrest.

77
00:09:23,312 --> 00:09:34,650
Handa and Laros (1993) diagnosed active-phase arrest, defined as no dilation for ≥2 hours, in 5 percent of term nulliparas.

78
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:38,662
This incidence has not changed since the 1950s .

79
00:09:38,662 --> 00:09:43,525
Inadequate uterine contractions, defined as <180

80
00:09:43,684 --> 00:09:52,509
Montevideo units, calculated as shown in Figure 23-1, were diagnosed in 80 percent of women with active-phase arrest.

81
00:09:52,459 --> 00:10:10,221
Hauth and coworkers (1986, 1991) reported that when labor is effectively induced or augmented with oxytocin, 90 percent of women achieve 200 to 225 Montevideo units, and 40 percent achieve at least 300 Montevideo units.

82
00:10:10,221 --> 00:10:18,309
These results suggest that certain minimums of uterine activity should be achieved before performing cesarean delivery for dystocia.

83
00:10:18,309 --> 00:10:23,384
Oxytocin regimens suitable to augment labor mirror those to induce labor.

84
00:10:23,384 --> 00:10:29,384
These regimens are outlined in detail in Chapter 26 (p. 493).

85
00:10:29,384 --> 00:10:31,921
Figure 23-1.

86
00:10:31,921 --> 00:10:43,934
Montevideo units are calculated by subtracting the baseline uterine pressure from the peak contraction pressure for each contraction in a 10-minute window and adding the pressures generated by each contraction.

87
00:10:43,934 --> 00:10:55,434
In the example shown, there were five contractions producing pressure changes of 52, 50, 47, 44, and 49 mm Hg, respectively.

88
00:10:55,434 --> 00:11:00,684
The sum of these five contractions is 242 Montevideo units.

89
00:11:00,684 --> 00:11:03,609
Other criteria should also be met.

90
00:11:03,609 --> 00:11:10,796
First, the latent phase should be completed, and the cervix is dilated ≥4 cm.

91
00:11:10,796 --> 00:11:22,634
Also, a uterine contraction pattern of ≥200 Montevideo units in a 10-minute period has been present for ≥4 hours without cervical change .

92
00:11:22,634 --> 00:11:27,496
The Consensus Committee has extended this further, as described next.

93
00:11:27,496 --> 00:11:30,459
Obstetric Care Consensus Committee.

94
00:11:30,459 --> 00:11:36,009
Four recommendations of the Consensus Committee apply to management of first-stage labor.

95
00:11:36,009 --> 00:11:40,659
The first admonishes against cesarean delivery in the latent phase.

96
00:11:40,659 --> 00:11:46,921
Specifically, a prolonged latent phase should not be the sole indication for cesarean delivery.

97
00:11:46,921 --> 00:11:51,134
This guideline is not new and is traceable to Friedman’s work.

98
00:11:51,134 --> 00:11:55,196
The second directive, too, is conventional practice.

99
00:11:55,196 --> 00:12:01,734
It recommends against cesarean delivery if labor is progressive but slow—a protraction disorder.

100
00:12:01,734 --> 00:12:09,921
This instance is typically managed with observation, assessment of uterine activity, and stimulation of contractions as needed.

101
00:12:09,921 --> 00:12:15,896
A third instruction addresses the cervical dilation threshold that serves to herald active labor.

102
00:12:15,896 --> 00:12:23,234
Namely, a cervical dilation of 6 cm—not 4 cm—is now the recommended threshold.

103
00:12:23,234 --> 00:12:29,321
Moreover, before this threshold, standards for active-phase progress should not be applied.

104
00:12:29,321 --> 00:12:36,271
Of note, the WHO (2018) recognizes 5 cm as the active-labor threshold.

105
00:12:36,271 --> 00:12:44,959
Other large studies noted labor acceleration after 5 cm (Ashwal, 2020; Oladapo, 2018).

106
00:12:44,959 --> 00:13:05,446
A fourth stipulation notes that cesarean delivery for active-phase arrest is best reserved for women with cervical dilation ≥6 cm and ruptured membranes who fail to progress despite 4 hours of adequate uterine activity or despite at least 6 hours of oxytocin administration but inadequate contractions.

107
00:13:05,446 --> 00:13:11,371
Of these, the change of thresholds from 4 to 6 cm has prompted the most scrutiny.

108
00:13:11,371 --> 00:13:22,634
Described in Chapter 22 (p. 424), original labor curves have been used since first proposed approximately 40 years ago by Friedman (1978).

109
00:13:22,634 --> 00:13:33,934
Instead, Zhang and coworkers (2002) proposed a new labor curve derived from a more contemporaneous cohort delivered between 1992 and 1996.

110
00:13:33,934 --> 00:13:43,184
The Friedman labor curves reflected women in spontaneous labor with infrequent use of neuraxial labor analgesia or oxytocin augmentation.

111
00:13:43,184 --> 00:13:51,284
In contrast, in the later cohort, approximately 50 percent of women had neuraxial analgesia or augmentation.

112
00:13:51,284 --> 00:13:58,559
In the later group, the rate of cervical change is slow between 4 and 6 cm but accelerates thereafter.

113
00:13:58,559 --> 00:14:04,134
This could reasonably be interpreted as the active phase beginning at 6 cm.

114
00:14:04,134 --> 00:14:11,721
In comparing both labor curves, the active phase curve in the Zhang cohort flattens beginning at 3 to 4 cm .

115
00:14:11,721 --> 00:14:21,046
Namely, the 6-cm rule for active labor derives from a slowing of the rate or flattening of the slope of cervical change in first-stage labor.

116
00:14:21,046 --> 00:14:23,559
Figure 23-2.

117
00:14:23,559 --> 00:14:29,834
Cervical dilation curves from Friedman (1955) and Zhang (2002).

118
00:14:29,834 --> 00:14:39,084
These findings by Zhang and coworkers (2002) are consistent with the labor results obtained in the subsequent Safe Labor Consortium study .

119
00:14:39,084 --> 00:14:47,046
However, this study derived its numbers from a retrospective observational dataset built from abstracted labor and delivery

120
00:14:47,212 --> 00:14:51,649
information from 19 hospitals across the United States.

121
00:14:51,599 --> 00:14:56,887
Various statistical methods and heavy manipulations of these numbers were used .

122
00:14:56,887 --> 00:15:04,387
Moreover, women were analyzed after excluding all women with cesarean deliveries or asphyxiated newborns.

123
00:15:04,387 --> 00:15:16,199
The Consensus Committee (2019b) was explicit that “the Consortium on Safe Labor data, rather than the standards proposed by Friedman, should inform evidence-based labor management.

124
00:15:16,199 --> 00:15:26,024
Critics of the Consensus Committee recommendations note that the Consortium on Safe Labor data were derived from clinical settings with a net cesarean rate of 30 percent.

125
00:15:26,024 --> 00:15:32,587
Thus, adherence to the new recommendations may fail to achieve desired cesarean rate reductions.

126
00:15:32,587 --> 00:15:39,899
Also, the study lacked a focus on neonatal safety, given that all the asphyxiated neonates were excluded.

127
00:15:39,899 --> 00:15:52,849
Supporters note that the study of prolonged first-stage labors by Cheng and coworkers (2010) found higher rates of cesarean delivery and chorioamnionitis but not higher rates of neonatal morbidity.

128
00:15:52,849 --> 00:16:02,237
However, Harper and associates (2014) analyzed maternal and neonatal adverse outcomes related to first-stage labor lengths.

129
00:16:02,237 --> 00:16:14,212
In 5030 women, first-stage labor durations were divided into those <90th percentile or those ≥90th percentile, with incremental increases thereafter.

130
00:16:14,212 --> 00:16:31,524
These authors and those of other large studies similarly found maternal and neonatal composite morbidity scores that were higher in cohorts after adoption of Consensus Committee guidelines compared with groups prior to implementation (Blankenship, 2020; Rosenbloom, 2017).

131
00:16:31,524 --> 00:16:41,374
This concern for adverse fetal and maternal effects resulting from the new Consensus Committee guidelines was echoed by Cohen and Friedman (2015a,b).

132
00:16:41,374 --> 00:16:47,799
Another caveat notes that the efficacy of these recommendations to achieve their primary goal is limited.

133
00:16:47,799 --> 00:17:01,699
One retrospective cohort study compared 3200 women with uncomplicated term pregnancy undergoing spontaneous or induced labor before guideline changes and 3000 similar gravidas after these changes.

134
00:17:01,699 --> 00:17:10,374
The cesarean delivery rate for first-stage labor arrest dropped from 1.8 to 0.9 percent in nulliparas during this time .

135
00:17:10,374 --> 00:17:14,199
Percent changes were not significant in multiparas.

136
00:17:14,199 --> 00:17:20,124
Adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes did not significantly differ between epoch groups.

137
00:17:20,124 --> 00:17:28,024
In another evaluation of outcomes before and after guideline implementation, cesarean delivery rates were unchanged .

138
00:17:28,024 --> 00:17:36,037
The Consensus Committee suggests that cesarean deliveries for dystocia are being done before 6 cm cervical dilation.

139
00:17:36,037 --> 00:17:48,137
However, evidence suggests that all of the Committee’s first-stage recommendations are actually already empirically in use but are concurrent with an overall cesarean delivery rate >30 percent.

140
00:17:48,137 --> 00:18:02,387
For example, Nelson and associates (2020) analyzed data from nearly 9000 women with primary cesarean deliveries for dystocia at 13 university hospitals between 1999 and 2000.

141
00:18:02,387 --> 00:18:09,087
Notably, the median cervical dilation at the time of cesarean for dystocia was 6 cm.

142
00:18:09,087 --> 00:18:19,499
As another example, primary cesarean delivery rates for dystocia at Parkland Hospital between 1988 and 2017 did not change significantly.

143
00:18:19,499 --> 00:18:25,749
Thus, Consensus Committee guidelines may fail to prevent additional cesareans for dystocia.

144
00:18:25,749 --> 00:18:28,812
Logically, further study is needed.

145
00:18:28,812 --> 00:18:38,012
Second-stage Descent Disorders As discussed in Chapter 22 (p. 425), fetal descent largely follows complete dilation.

146
00:18:38,012 --> 00:18:45,887
Moreover, the second stage incorporates many of the cardinal movements necessary for the fetus to negotiate the birth canal.

147
00:18:45,887 --> 00:18:52,399
Thus, disproportion of the fetus and pelvis frequently becomes apparent during second-stage labor.

148
00:18:52,399 --> 00:19:01,562
Similar to first-stage labor, time boundaries have been supported to limit second-stage duration to minimize adverse maternal and fetal outcomes.

149
00:19:01,562 --> 00:19:09,562
The second stage in nulliparas has been limited to 2 hours and extended to 3 hours when regional analgesia is used.

150
00:19:09,562 --> 00:19:16,187
For multiparas, 1 hour has been the limit, extended to 2 hours with regional analgesia.

151
00:19:16,187 --> 00:19:26,062
Several studies supported this extension (Cohen, 1977; Menticoglou, 1995a,b; Saunders, 1992).

152
00:19:26,062 --> 00:19:32,087
However, of maternal outcomes, higher rates of chorioamnionitis, anal sphincter

153
00:19:32,236 --> 00:19:42,173
injury, operative vaginal birth, and postpartum hemorrhage accrue as the second stage lengthens (Allen, 2009; Rouse, 2009).

154
00:19:42,123 --> 00:19:49,048
Newer guidelines have been promoted by the Consensus Committee (2019b) for second-stage labor.

155
00:19:49,048 --> 00:19:58,411
These recommend that a nullipara push for at least 3 hours and a multipara push for at least 2 hours before second-stage labor arrest is diagnosed.

156
00:19:58,411 --> 00:20:03,911
Importantly, one caveat is that maternal and fetal status should be reassuring.

157
00:20:03,911 --> 00:20:09,611
These authors provide options to these times before cesarean delivery is performed.

158
00:20:09,611 --> 00:20:15,086
Namely, longer durations may be appropriate as long as progress is documented.

159
00:20:15,086 --> 00:20:24,573
Also, a specific maximal length of time spent in second-stage labor beyond which all women should undergo operative delivery has not been identified.

160
00:20:24,573 --> 00:20:31,811
Intuitively, the goal to lower cesarean delivery rates is best balanced with one to ensure neonatal safety.

161
00:20:31,811 --> 00:20:39,523
It is problematic that no robust data on neonatal outcomes support the safety of allowing prolonged second-stage labor.

162
00:20:39,523 --> 00:20:55,286
Data from many evaluations reveal that serious newborn consequences attend second-stage labors longer than 3 hours (Bleich, 2012; Cahill, 2018; Nelson, 2020; Rosenbloom, 2017).

163
00:20:55,286 --> 00:21:09,086
Other data, when adjusted for labor variables, show no difference in adverse neonatal complications for these longer second stages (Cheng, 2004; Le Ray, 2009; Rouse, 2009).

164
00:21:09,086 --> 00:21:22,061
Some investigators have argued that the absolute number of such outcomes is small and overall outcomes remain good (Grantz, 2018; Grobman, 2016; Laughon, 2014).

165
00:21:22,061 --> 00:21:25,811
That said, some of the adverse outcomes are severe.

166
00:21:25,811 --> 00:21:33,086
To fully ascertain specific effect of these guidelines on morbidity rates, randomized controlled trials are needed.

167
00:21:33,086 --> 00:21:38,348
It is possible that prolonged first-stage labor presages that with the second stage.

168
00:21:38,348 --> 00:21:51,136
Nelson and coworkers (2013) studied the relationships between the lengths of the first and second stages of labor in 12,523 nulliparas at term delivered at Parkland Hospital.

169
00:21:51,136 --> 00:21:57,136
The second stage significantly lengthened concomitantly with increasing first-stage duration.

170
00:21:57,136 --> 00:22:04,798
The 95th percentile was 15.6 and 2.9 hours for the first and second stages, respectively.

171
00:22:04,798 --> 00:22:17,336
Women with first stages lasting longer than 15.6 hours (>95th percentile) had a 16-percent rate of second-stage labor lasting 3 hours (95th percentile).

172
00:22:17,336 --> 00:22:25,986
This compared with a 4.5-percent rate of prolonged second stages in women with first-stage labors lasting <95th percentile.

173
00:22:25,986 --> 00:22:28,898
# Maternal Pushing Efforts.

174
00:22:28,898 --> 00:22:35,486
With full cervical dilation, most women cannot resist the urge to push with uterine contractions.

175
00:22:35,486 --> 00:22:42,511
The combined force created by contractions of the uterus and abdominal musculature propels the fetus downward.

176
00:22:42,511 --> 00:22:52,061
However, at times, force created by abdominal musculature is compromised sufficiently to slow or even prevent spontaneous vaginal delivery.

177
00:22:52,061 --> 00:23:01,311
Heavy sedation or regional analgesia may reduce the reflex urge to push and may impair the ability to contract abdominal muscles sufficiently.

178
00:23:01,311 --> 00:23:04,611
Allowing time for these to abate is reasonable.

179
00:23:04,611 --> 00:23:10,986
In other instances, the urge to push is overridden by the intense pain created by bearing down.

180
00:23:10,986 --> 00:23:23,261
Depending on fetal station and anticipated second stage, options include emotional support and encouragement, parenteral analgesia, pudendal blockade, or neuraxial analgesia.

181
00:23:23,261 --> 00:23:26,423
Prematurely Ruptured Membranes At Term.

182
00:23:26,423 --> 00:23:33,986
Membrane rupture at term without spontaneous uterine contractions complicates approximately 8 percent of pregnancies.

183
00:23:33,986 --> 00:23:40,948
In the past, labor stimulation was initiated if contractions did not begin after 6 to 12 hours.

184
00:23:40,948 --> 00:23:55,686
Practice-changing research included that of Hannah (1996) and Peleg (1999) and their associates, who enrolled a total of 5042 pregnancies with ruptured membranes in a randomized investigation.

185
00:23:55,686 --> 00:24:05,586
They measured the effects of induction versus expectant management and also compared induction using intravenous oxytocin with that using prostaglandin E2 gel.

186
00:24:05,586 --> 00:24:11,311
There were approximately 1200 pregnancies in each of the four study arms.

187
00:24:11,311 --> 00:24:16,898
They concluded that labor induction with intravenous oxytocin was preferred management.

188
00:24:16,898 --> 00:24:22,961
This was based on significantly fewer intrapartum and postpartum infections in women whose

189
00:24:23,116 --> 00:24:25,241
labor was induced.

190
00:24:25,191 --> 00:24:29,678
There were no significant differences in cesarean delivery rates.

191
00:24:29,678 --> 00:24:40,778
Subsequent analysis by Hannah and coworkers (2000) indicated higher rates of adverse outcomes when expectant management at home was compared with in-hospital observation.

192
00:24:40,778 --> 00:24:56,103
Mozurkewich and associates (2009) reported lower rates of chorioamnionitis, metritis, and neonatal intensive care unit admissions for women with term ruptured membranes whose labors were induced compared with those managed expectantly.

193
00:24:56,103 --> 00:25:02,903
At Parkland Hospital, labor is induced soon after admission when ruptured membranes are confirmed at term.

194
00:25:02,903 --> 00:25:11,903
In those with hypotonic contractions or with advanced cervical dilation, oxytocin is selected to lower potential hyperstimulation risk.

195
00:25:11,903 --> 00:25:25,603
In those with an unfavorable cervix, no or few contraction, and no significant fetal heart rate decelerations, prostaglandin E1 (misoprostol) is chosen to promote cervical ripening and contractions.

196
00:25:25,603 --> 00:25:32,528
The benefit of prophylactic antibiotics in women with ruptured membranes before labor at term is unclear .

197
00:25:32,528 --> 00:25:42,116
However, in those with membranes ruptured longer than 18 hours, antibiotics are instituted for group B streptococcal infection prophylaxis .

198
00:25:42,116 --> 00:25:44,791
Precipitous Labor And Delivery.

199
00:25:44,791 --> 00:25:49,378
Labor can be too slow, but it also can be abnormally rapid.

200
00:25:49,378 --> 00:25:54,328
Precipitous labor and delivery is extremely rapid labor and delivery.

201
00:25:54,328 --> 00:26:07,366
It may result from an abnormally low resistance of the soft parts of the birth canal, from abnormally strong uterine and abdominal contractions, or rarely from a lack of pain with contractions to cue advanced labor.

202
00:26:07,366 --> 00:26:12,691
Precipitous labor terminates in expulsion of the fetus in <3 hours.

203
00:26:12,691 --> 00:26:23,491
Using this definition, 25,260 live births—3 percent—were complicated by precipitous labor in the United States in 2013 .

204
00:26:23,491 --> 00:26:29,666
Despite this incidence, little information is published on maternal and perinatal outcomes.

205
00:26:29,666 --> 00:26:40,128
For the mother, complications are few if the cervix is effaced appreciably and compliant, if the vagina has been stretched previously, and if the perineum is relaxed.

206
00:26:40,128 --> 00:26:53,703
Conversely, vigorous uterine contractions combined with a long, firm cervix and a noncompliant birth canal may lead to uterine rupture or extensive lacerations of the cervix, vagina, vulva, or perineum .

207
00:26:53,703 --> 00:26:59,553
It is in these latter circumstances that amnionic-fluid embolism most likely develops .

208
00:26:59,553 --> 00:27:03,653
Precipitous labor is frequently followed by uterine atony.

209
00:27:03,653 --> 00:27:13,141
In one report of 99 term pregnancies, short labors were more common in multiparas who typically had contractions at intervals less than 2 minutes.

210
00:27:13,141 --> 00:27:22,653
Precipitous labors have been linked to cocaine abuse and associated with placental abruption, meconium, postpartum hemorrhage, and low Apgar scores .

211
00:27:22,653 --> 00:27:29,941
For the neonate, adverse perinatal outcomes from rapid labor may be increased considerably for several reasons.

212
00:27:29,941 --> 00:27:39,028
The uterine contractions, often with negligible intervals of relaxation, prevent appropriate uterine blood flow and fetal oxygenation.

213
00:27:39,028 --> 00:27:44,966
Related to trauma, resistance of the birth canal rarely may cause intracranial injury.

214
00:27:44,966 --> 00:27:53,428
In one review of 22 cases of Erb or Duchenne palsy, precipitous second-stage labor was associated in a third of the cases .

215
00:27:53,428 --> 00:27:58,391
During unattended birth, the newborn may fall to the floor and be injured.

216
00:27:58,391 --> 00:28:04,303
Last, needed resuscitation may not be immediately available due to delivery speed.

217
00:28:04,303 --> 00:28:10,653
As treatment, analgesia is unlikely to modify these forceful contractions significantly.

218
00:28:10,653 --> 00:28:17,503
The use of tocolytic agents such as magnesium sulfate or terbutaline is unproven in these circumstances.

219
00:28:17,503 --> 00:28:26,578
A single, intramuscular 250ug terbutaline dose may be reasonable in an attempt to resolve a nonreassuring fetal heart rate pattern.

220
00:28:26,578 --> 00:28:32,328
This is balanced against the risk of associated uterine atony if delivery is imminent.

221
00:28:32,328 --> 00:28:36,391
Certainly, oxytocin administration should be stopped.

222
00:28:36,391 --> 00:28:38,866
Fetopelvic Disproportion.

223
00:28:38,866 --> 00:28:41,478
# Pelvic Capacity.

224
00:28:41,478 --> 00:28:50,616
Fetopelvic disproportion arises from diminished pelvic capacity or from abnormal fetal size, structure, presentation, or position.

225
00:28:50,616 --> 00:28:53,166
Commonly, both are present.

226
00:28:53,166 --> 00:28:59,666
The pelvic inlet, midpelvis, or pelvic outlet may be contracted solely or in combination.

227
00:28:59,666 --> 00:29:03,128
Any contraction of the pelvic diameters that

228
00:29:03,292 --> 00:29:07,042
diminishes pelvic capacity can create dystocia.

229
00:29:06,992 --> 00:29:12,442
Normal pelvic anatomy is also discussed in Chapter 2 (p. 28).

230
00:29:12,442 --> 00:29:20,779
Contracted Inlet Before labor, the fetal biparietal diameter averages from 9.5 to 9.8 cm.

231
00:29:20,779 --> 00:29:48,192
Thus, it might prove difficult or even impossible for some fetuses to pass through a pelvic inlet that has an anteroposterior diameter <10 cm. Mengert (1948) and Kaltreider (1952), employing x-ray pelvimetry, demonstrated that the incidence of difficult deliveries rises when either the anteroposterior diameter of the inlet is <10 cm or the transverse diameter is <12 cm.

232
00:29:48,192 --> 00:29:52,342
Either threshold can be used to consider a pelvis contracted.

233
00:29:52,342 --> 00:29:59,567
As expected, when both diameters are shortened, dystocia rates are much greater than when only one is diminished.

234
00:29:59,567 --> 00:30:05,242
The anteroposterior diameter of the inlet is also called the obstetrical conjugate.

235
00:30:05,242 --> 00:30:13,504
It is commonly approximated by manually measuring the diagonal conjugate, which is approximately 1.5 cm greater.

236
00:30:13,504 --> 00:30:21,067
To measure the diagonal conjugate, a hand with the palm oriented laterally extends its index finger to the promontory.

237
00:30:21,067 --> 00:30:29,142
The distance from the fingertip to the point at which the lowest margin of the symphysis strikes the same finger’s base is the diagonal conjugate.

238
00:30:29,142 --> 00:30:35,854
Inlet contraction usually is defined as a diagonal conjugate <11.5 cm.

239
00:30:35,854 --> 00:30:42,304
A diminutive woman is likely to have a small pelvis, but she is also likely to have a small neonate.

240
00:30:42,304 --> 00:30:56,979
Thoms (1937) studied 362 nulliparas and found that the mean birthweight of their offspring was significantly lower— 280 g—in women with a small pelvis than in those with a medium or large pelvis.

241
00:30:56,979 --> 00:31:08,004
Normally, cervical dilation is aided by hydrostatic action of the unruptured membranes or by direct application of the presenting part against the cervix after membrane rupture.

242
00:31:08,004 --> 00:31:20,417
In contracted pelves, however, because the head is arrested in the pelvic inlet, the entire force exerted by contractions acts directly on the portion of membranes that contact the dilating cervix.

243
00:31:20,417 --> 00:31:25,429
Consequently, early spontaneous rupture of the membranes is more likely.

244
00:31:25,429 --> 00:31:34,217
After membrane rupture, absent pressure by the head against the cervix and lower uterine segment predisposes to less effective contractions.

245
00:31:34,217 --> 00:31:38,904
Hence, further dilation may proceed very slowly or not at all.

246
00:31:38,904 --> 00:31:50,817
Cibils and Hendricks (1965) reported that the mechanical adaptation of the fetal passenger to the bony passage plays an important part in determining the efficiency of contractions.

247
00:31:50,817 --> 00:31:54,829
Better adaptation begets more efficient contractions.

248
00:31:54,829 --> 00:32:00,942
A contracted inlet also plays an important part in the production of abnormal presentations.

249
00:32:00,942 --> 00:32:09,479
In nulliparas with normal pelvic capacity, the presenting part at term commonly descends into the pelvic cavity before labor onset.

250
00:32:09,479 --> 00:32:18,817
If the inlet is contracted considerably or if asynclitism is marked, descent usually does not take place until after labor onset, if at all.

251
00:32:18,817 --> 00:32:27,454
Cephalic presentations still predominate, but the head floats over the pelvic inlet or rests more laterally in one of the iliac fossae.

252
00:32:27,454 --> 00:32:33,404
Accordingly, very slight influences may cause the fetus to assume other presentations.

253
00:32:33,404 --> 00:32:41,967
In women with contracted pelves, face and shoulder presentations are encountered more frequently, and the cord prolapses more often.

254
00:32:41,967 --> 00:32:46,942
Contracted Midpelvis This finding is more common than inlet contraction.

255
00:32:46,942 --> 00:32:55,979
It frequently causes transverse arrest of the fetal head, which potentially can lead to a difficult midforceps operation or to cesarean delivery.

256
00:32:55,979 --> 00:33:06,679
The obstetrical plane of the midpelvis extends from the inferior margin of the symphysis pubis through the ischial spines and touches the sacrum near the junction of the fourth and fifth vertebrae.

257
00:33:06,679 --> 00:33:17,717
A transverse line theoretically connecting the ischial spines divides the midpelvis into anterior and posterior portions (Fig. 2-16, p. 29).

258
00:33:17,717 --> 00:33:25,129
The former is bounded anteriorly by the lower border of the symphysis pubis and laterally by the ischiopubic rami.

259
00:33:25,129 --> 00:33:34,479
The posterior portion is bounded dorsally by the sacrum and laterally by the sacrospinous ligaments, forming the lower limits of the sacrosciatic notch.

260
00:33:34,479 --> 00:33:38,817
Average midpelvis measurements are as follows: transverse,

261
00:33:38,980 --> 00:33:58,355
or interischial spinous, 10.5 cm; anteroposterior, from the lower border of the symphysis pubis to the junction of S4 and S5, 11.5 cm; and posterior sagittal, from the midpoint of the interspinous line to the same point on the sacrum, 5 cm.

262
00:33:58,355 --> 00:34:05,830
The definition of midpelvic contractions has not been established with the same precision possible for inlet contractions.

263
00:34:05,830 --> 00:34:23,142
Even so, the midpelvis is likely contracted when the sum of the interspinous and posterior sagittal diameters of the midpelvis—normally, 10.5 plus 5 cm, or 15.5 cm—falls to 13.5 cm or less.

264
00:34:23,142 --> 00:34:31,080
This concept was emphasized by Chen and Huang (1982) in evaluating possible midpelvic contraction.

265
00:34:31,080 --> 00:34:37,580
Midpelvic contraction is suspected whenever the interspinous diameter is <10 cm.

266
00:34:37,580 --> 00:34:42,530
When it measures <8 cm, the midpelvis is contracted.

267
00:34:42,530 --> 00:34:56,167
Although no precise manual method permits measure of midpelvic dimensions, a suggestion of contraction sometimes can be inferred if the spines are prominent, the pelvic sidewalls converge, or the sacrosciatic notch is narrow.

268
00:34:56,167 --> 00:35:12,242
Moreover, Eller and Mengert (1947) noted that the relationship between the intertuberous and interspinous diameters of the ischium is sufficiently constant that narrowing of the interspinous diameter can be anticipated when the intertuberous diameter is narrow.

269
00:35:12,242 --> 00:35:19,217
A normal intertuberous diameter, however, does not always exclude a narrow interspinous diameter.

270
00:35:19,217 --> 00:35:26,755
Contracted Outlet This finding usually is defined as an interischial tuberous diameter of 8 cm or less.

271
00:35:26,755 --> 00:35:34,917
The pelvic outlet may be roughly likened to two triangles, with the interischial tuberous diameter constituting the base of both.

272
00:35:34,917 --> 00:35:43,105
The sides of the anterior triangle are the pubic rami, and its apex is the inferoposterior surface of the symphysis pubis.

273
00:35:43,105 --> 00:35:51,880
The posterior triangle has no bony sides but is limited at its apex by the tip of the last sacral vertebra—not the tip of the coccyx.

274
00:35:51,880 --> 00:36:00,767
Diminution of the intertuberous diameter with consequent narrowing of the anterior triangle must inevitably force the fetal head posteriorly.

275
00:36:00,767 --> 00:36:11,780
Floberg and associates (1987) reported that outlet contractions were found in almost 1 percent of more than 1400 unselected nulliparas with term pregnancies.

276
00:36:11,780 --> 00:36:19,342
A contracted outlet may cause dystocia not so much by itself but by an often-associated midpelvic contraction.

277
00:36:19,342 --> 00:36:23,967
Outlet contraction without concomitant midplane contraction is rare.

278
00:36:23,967 --> 00:36:34,617
Although the disproportion between the fetal head and the pelvic outlet is not sufficiently great to give rise to severe dystocia, it may play an important part in perineal tears.

279
00:36:34,617 --> 00:36:44,505
With increased narrowing of the pubic arch, the occiput cannot emerge directly beneath the symphysis pubis but is forced farther down upon the ischiopubic rami.

280
00:36:44,505 --> 00:36:51,530
The perineum, consequently, becomes increasingly distended and thus exposed to risk of laceration.

281
00:36:51,530 --> 00:37:00,492
Pelvic Fractures Vallier (2012) and Riehl (2014) reviewed experiences with pelvic fractures and pregnancy.

282
00:37:00,492 --> 00:37:04,680
Trauma from automobile collisions was the most common cause.

283
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:13,742
Moreover, they note that fracture pattern, minor malalignment, and retained hardware are not absolute indications for cesarean delivery.

284
00:37:13,742 --> 00:37:22,705
In determining vaginal delivery candidates, fracture healing requires 8 to 12 weeks and thus recent fracture merits cesarean delivery .

285
00:37:22,705 --> 00:37:30,055
With a healed fracture, care includes review of pelvic radiographs and possible pelvimetry later in pregnancy.

286
00:37:30,055 --> 00:37:32,980
# Radiologic Assessment.

287
00:37:32,980 --> 00:37:39,792
Current evaluation of pelvic capacity typically uses only digital interrogation of the bony pelvis.

288
00:37:39,792 --> 00:37:52,417
Of radiological methods, x-ray pelvimetry with cephalic presentations provides poor predictive value to diagnose CPD (Mengert, 1948; Pattinson, 2017).

289
00:37:52,417 --> 00:38:04,705
Similarly, magnetic resonance pelvimetry fails to provide suitable accuracy in predicting cesarean delivery for dystocia (Sporri, 2002; Zaretsky, 2005).

290
00:38:04,705 --> 00:38:08,680
Fetal size alone is seldom the reason for failed labor.

291
00:38:08,680 --> 00:38:16,592
Indeed, most cases of CPD arise in fetuses whose weight is well within the range of the general obstetrical population.

292
00:38:16,592 --> 00:38:26,430
Figure 23-3 depicts Parkland Hospital data, in which two thirds of neonates who required cesarean delivery after failed forceps delivery

293
00:38:26,596 --> 00:38:30,096
weighed <3700 g.

294
00:38:30,046 --> 00:38:35,521
Thus, head malposition more likely obstructs fetal passage through the birth canal.

295
00:38:35,521 --> 00:38:43,058
These include asynclitism, occiput posterior position, and face, brow, or sinciput presentation.

296
00:38:43,058 --> 00:38:45,583
Figure 23-3.

297
00:38:45,583 --> 00:38:56,746
Birthweight distribution of 362 newborns born by cesarean delivery after a failed forceps attempt at Parkland Hospital from 1989–1999.

298
00:38:56,746 --> 00:39:04,146
Only 12 percent (n = 44) of the newborns weighed >4000 g (dark bars).

299
00:39:04,146 --> 00:39:12,208
For fetal head size estimation, clinical and radiographical methods to predict CPD also have proved disappointing.

300
00:39:12,208 --> 00:39:25,933
First, Mueller (1885) and Hillis (1930) described a clinical maneuver in which the fetal head is grasped through the abdominal wall, and firm pressure is directed downward along the axis of the inlet.

301
00:39:25,933 --> 00:39:32,746
If no disproportion exists, the head readily enters the pelvis, and vaginal delivery can be predicted.

302
00:39:32,746 --> 00:39:40,358
However, a prospective evaluation of this Mueller-Hillis maneuver found it poorly predicted subsequent labor dystocia .

303
00:39:40,358 --> 00:39:47,583
Measurements of fetal head diameters using plain radiographical techniques are hindered by parallax distortions.

304
00:39:47,583 --> 00:39:59,646
The biparietal diameter and head circumference can be measured sonographically, and Thurnau and colleagues (1991) used the fetal-pelvic index to identify labor complications.

305
00:39:59,646 --> 00:40:09,046
However, the sensitivity of such measurements to predict CPD is poor (Ferguson, 1998; Korhonen, 2015).

306
00:40:09,046 --> 00:40:11,708
# Face Presentation.

307
00:40:11,708 --> 00:40:22,133
Etiology and Diagnosis With this presentation, the neck is hyperextended so that the occiput is in contact with the fetal back, and the chin (mentum) is presenting .

308
00:40:22,133 --> 00:40:30,308
The rate is approximately 0.1 percent of births (Arsène, 2019; Gardberg, 2011).

309
00:40:30,308 --> 00:40:32,983
Figure 23-4.

310
00:40:32,983 --> 00:40:35,121
Face presentation.

311
00:40:35,121 --> 00:40:37,871
The chin is directly posterior.

312
00:40:37,871 --> 00:40:42,771
Vaginal delivery is impossible unless the chin rotates anteriorly.

313
00:40:42,771 --> 00:40:49,746
Causes of face presentations are numerous and include conditions that favor neck extension or prevent flexion.

314
00:40:49,746 --> 00:41:03,708
Preterm fetuses, with their smaller head dimensions, can engage before conversion to occiput presentation, and multifetal gestations carry increased risk (Arsène, 2019; Shaffer, 2006).

315
00:41:03,708 --> 00:41:07,146
High parity is another predisposing factor .

316
00:41:07,146 --> 00:41:15,758
In these cases, a pendulous maternal abdomen permits the fetal back to sag forward, which promotes extension of the cervical spine.

317
00:41:15,758 --> 00:41:25,621
Fetal malformations and hydramnios are other risk factors for face or brow presentations, and anencephalic fetuses naturally present by the face .

318
00:41:25,621 --> 00:41:33,071
Last, extended neck positions develop more frequently when the pelvis is contracted or the fetus is very large.

319
00:41:33,071 --> 00:41:43,183
In a series of 141 face presentations studied by Hellman and coworkers (1950), the incidence of inlet contraction was 40 percent.

320
00:41:43,183 --> 00:41:48,896
This high incidence of pelvic contraction should be kept in mind when considering management.

321
00:41:48,896 --> 00:41:55,108
Face presentation is diagnosed by vaginal examination and palpation of facial features.

322
00:41:55,108 --> 00:41:59,583
Notably, a breech may be mistaken for a face presentation.

323
00:41:59,583 --> 00:42:09,758
With careful examination, however, the finger encounters muscular resistance with the anus, whereas the bony, less-yielding jaws and palate are felt through the mouth.

324
00:42:09,758 --> 00:42:14,783
The finger, upon removal from the anus, may be stained with meconium.

325
00:42:14,783 --> 00:42:22,508
The mouth and malar eminences form a triangular shape, whereas the ischial tuberosities and anus lie in a straight line.

326
00:42:22,508 --> 00:42:25,733
Sonography can aid unclear cases .

327
00:42:25,733 --> 00:42:33,258
Used rarely, radiographs demonstrate a hyperextended head with the facial bones at or below the pelvic inlet .

328
00:42:33,258 --> 00:42:43,621
Mechanism of Labor The fetal face may present with the chin (mentum) anteriorly, transversely, or posteriorly, relative to the maternal symphysis pubis .

329
00:42:43,621 --> 00:42:57,233
Although some mentum posterior presentations persist, most convert spontaneously to an anterior position, even as late as second-stage labor (Duff, 1981; Sharshiner, 2015).

330
00:42:57,233 --> 00:43:03,683
With the chin anterior, internal rotation of the face brings the chin under the symphysis pubis .

331
00:43:03,683 --> 00:43:09,196
Only in this way can the neck traverse the posterior surface of the symphysis pubis.

332
00:43:09,196 --> 00:43:17,896
After anterior rotation and descent, the chin and mouth appear at the vulva, and the undersurface of the chin presses against the symphysis.

333
00:43:17,896 --> 00:43:21,933
Once the chin clears the symphysis, the neck can flex.

334
00:43:21,933 --> 00:43:25,171
The nose, eyes, brow, and

335
00:43:25,324 --> 00:43:30,349
occiput then appear in succession over the anterior margin of the perineum.

336
00:43:30,299 --> 00:43:35,049
After birth of the head, the occiput sags backward toward the anus.

337
00:43:35,049 --> 00:43:43,936
Next, the chin rotates externally to the side toward which it was originally directed, and the shoulders are born as in cephalic presentations.

338
00:43:43,936 --> 00:43:49,461
Fortunately temporary, edema and bruising can significantly distort the face.

339
00:43:49,461 --> 00:43:52,149
Figure 23-5.

340
00:43:52,149 --> 00:43:54,286
Face presentation.

341
00:43:54,286 --> 00:43:58,386
A. Mechanism of labor for right mentoanterior position.

342
00:43:58,386 --> 00:44:04,474
B. Swollen eyes and lips are common and transient in newborns delivered with face presentation.

343
00:44:04,474 --> 00:44:12,299
Instead, if the chin persists posteriorly, the relatively short neck cannot span the anterior surface of the sacrum.

344
00:44:12,299 --> 00:44:17,561
Moreover, the fetal brow is pressed against the maternal symphysis pubis.

345
00:44:17,561 --> 00:44:22,574
This position precludes the flexion necessary to negotiate the birth canal.

346
00:44:22,574 --> 00:44:36,099
Hence, as noted earlier, vaginal birth from a mentum posterior position is impossible unless the shoulders enter the pelvis at the same time, an event that is impossible except for an extremely small or macerated fetus.

347
00:44:36,099 --> 00:44:44,186
Management During labor, fetal heart rate monitoring is best done with external devices to help avoid face or eye injury.

348
00:44:44,186 --> 00:44:56,074
Because face presentations among term-size fetuses are more common with some degree of pelvic inlet contraction, cesarean delivery rates are substantially higher than with occiput presentation.

349
00:44:56,074 --> 00:45:03,249
If indicated, low or outlet forceps delivery of a mentum anterior face presentation can be completed .

350
00:45:03,249 --> 00:45:08,286
Vacuum extraction has been associated with eye trauma and is not recommended .

351
00:45:08,286 --> 00:45:13,661
As noted, rotation to a mentum anterior position may occur late in labor.

352
00:45:13,661 --> 00:45:16,711
Conversion methods should not be pursued.

353
00:45:16,711 --> 00:45:31,124
Namely, attempts to convert a face presentation manually to an occiput one, to rotate a posterior chin to a mentum anterior position, or to complete internal podalic version and extraction are dangerous and not recommended.

354
00:45:31,124 --> 00:45:33,724
# Brow Presentation.

355
00:45:33,724 --> 00:45:43,049
This uncommon presentation is diagnosed when that portion of the fetal head between the orbital ridge and the anterior fontanel presents at the pelvic inlet.

356
00:45:43,049 --> 00:45:52,774
As shown in Figure 23-6, the fetal head thus occupies a position midway between full flexion (occiput) and full extension (face).

357
00:45:52,774 --> 00:46:01,636
Rates range from 0.1 to 0.2 percent of births (Gardberg, 2011; Verspyck, 2012).

358
00:46:01,636 --> 00:46:04,349
Figure 23-6.

359
00:46:04,349 --> 00:46:07,074
Brow posterior presentation.

360
00:46:07,074 --> 00:46:12,224
Risks for persistent brow presentation mirror those for face presentation.

361
00:46:12,224 --> 00:46:18,574
A brow presentation is commonly unstable and converts to a face or an occiput presentation .

362
00:46:18,574 --> 00:46:28,974
The presentation may be recognized by abdominal palpation when both the occiput and chin can be palpated easily, but vaginal examination is usually necessary.

363
00:46:28,974 --> 00:46:40,099
The frontal sutures, large anterior fontanel, orbital ridges, eyes, and root of the nose are felt during vaginal examination, but neither the mouth nor the chin is palpable.

364
00:46:40,099 --> 00:46:43,374
Sonographic landmarks have been described .

365
00:46:43,374 --> 00:46:54,311
Except when the fetal head is small or the pelvis is unusually large, engagement of the fetal head and subsequent delivery cannot take place as long as the brow presentation persists.

366
00:46:54,311 --> 00:47:06,674
Engagement is impossible until marked molding shortens the occipitomental diameter or, more commonly, until the neck either flexes to an occiput presentation or extends to a face presentation.

367
00:47:06,674 --> 00:47:13,599
The considerable molding essential for vaginal delivery of a persistent brow characteristically deforms the head.

368
00:47:13,599 --> 00:47:21,799
The caput succedaneum is over the forehead, and it may be so extensive that identification of the brow by palpation is impossible.

369
00:47:21,799 --> 00:47:28,611
In these instances, the forehead is prominent and squared, and the occipitomental diameter is diminished.

370
00:47:28,611 --> 00:47:32,699
Management principles mirror those for a face presentation.

371
00:47:32,699 --> 00:47:35,261
# Transverse Lie.

372
00:47:35,261 --> 00:47:42,599
Etiology and Diagnosis With this, the fetus’ long axis lies approximately perpendicular to that of the mother.

373
00:47:42,599 --> 00:47:47,836
In a transverse lie, the shoulder is usually positioned over the pelvic inlet.

374
00:47:47,836 --> 00:47:52,361
The head occupies one iliac fossa, and the breech the other.

375
00:47:52,361 --> 00:48:01,824
This creates a shoulder presentation in which the side of the mother on which the acromion rests determines the designation of the position as right or left acromial.

376
00:48:01,824 --> 00:48:03,874
In addition, the

377
00:48:04,036 --> 00:48:10,348
back may be directed anteriorly or posteriorly and also superiorly or inferiorly.

378
00:48:10,298 --> 00:48:17,711
Thus, it is customary to further distinguish right or left varieties as dorsoanterior and dorsoposterior .

379
00:48:17,711 --> 00:48:20,373
Figure 23-7.

380
00:48:20,373 --> 00:48:22,323
Transverse lie.

381
00:48:22,323 --> 00:48:27,286
Leopold maneuver of a fetus with right acromiodorsoanterior position.

382
00:48:27,286 --> 00:48:36,623
Transverse lie complicates approximately 0.3 percent of births (Cruikshank, 1973; Gemer, 1994).

383
00:48:36,623 --> 00:48:49,773
Some of the more common causes include abdominal wall relaxation from high parity, preterm fetus, placenta previa, abnormal uterine anatomy, hydramnios, and contracted maternal pelvis.

384
00:48:49,773 --> 00:48:55,311
A transverse lie is usually recognized easily, often by inspection alone.

385
00:48:55,311 --> 00:49:02,373
The abdomen is unusually wide, whereas the uterine fundus extends to only slightly above the umbilicus.

386
00:49:02,373 --> 00:49:09,348
No fetal pole is detected in the fundus, and the ballottable head is found on one side and the breech on the other.

387
00:49:09,348 --> 00:49:15,198
When the back is anterior, a hard resistance plane extends across the front of the abdomen.

388
00:49:15,198 --> 00:49:22,536
When it is posterior, irregular nodules that represent fetal small parts are felt through the mother’s abdominal wall.

389
00:49:22,536 --> 00:49:31,436
During vaginal examination, in the early stages of labor, if the side of the thorax can be reached, the sequential parallel ribs are felt.

390
00:49:31,436 --> 00:49:38,198
With further dilation, the scapula and the clavicle are distinguished on opposite sides of the thorax.

391
00:49:38,198 --> 00:49:43,886
The position of the axilla indicates the side of the mother toward which the shoulder is directed.

392
00:49:43,886 --> 00:49:51,286
Mechanism of Labor Spontaneous delivery of a fully developed newborn is impossible with a persistent transverse lie.

393
00:49:51,286 --> 00:50:00,686
After rupture of the membranes, if labor continues, the fetal shoulder is forced into the pelvis, and the corresponding arm frequently prolapses.

394
00:50:00,686 --> 00:50:05,961
After some descent, the shoulder is arrested by the margins of the pelvic inlet.

395
00:50:05,961 --> 00:50:11,661
As labor continues, the shoulder is impacted firmly in the upper part of the pelvis.

396
00:50:11,661 --> 00:50:17,673
The uterus then contracts vigorously in an unsuccessful attempt to overcome the obstacle.

397
00:50:17,673 --> 00:50:23,723
With time, a uterine contraction ring rises increasingly higher and becomes more marked.

398
00:50:23,723 --> 00:50:28,761
An extreme form is the Bandl ring, described in the complication section.

399
00:50:28,761 --> 00:50:33,486
With a neglected transverse lie, the uterus will eventually rupture.

400
00:50:33,486 --> 00:50:47,948
Even without this complication, maternal and fetal morbidity rates with transverse lie are increased because of the frequent association with placenta previa, umbilical cord prolapse, and fetal manipulations during cesarean delivery.

401
00:50:47,948 --> 00:50:58,061
If the fetus is small—usually <800 g—and the pelvis is large, spontaneous delivery is possible despite persistence of the abnormal lie.

402
00:50:58,061 --> 00:51:02,098
The fetus is compressed with the head forced against its abdomen.

403
00:51:02,098 --> 00:51:08,761
A portion of the thoracic wall below the shoulder thus becomes the most dependent part, appearing at the vulva.

404
00:51:08,761 --> 00:51:13,373
The head and thorax then pass through the pelvic cavity at the same time.

405
00:51:13,373 --> 00:51:20,673
The fetus, which is doubled upon itself in a position sometimes referred to as conduplicato corpore, is expelled.

406
00:51:20,673 --> 00:51:26,748
Management Active labor in a woman with a transverse lie typically requires cesarean delivery.

407
00:51:26,748 --> 00:51:34,248
With dorsoanterior or back down position, neither the fetal feet nor head occupies the lower uterine segment.

408
00:51:34,248 --> 00:51:39,148
A low transverse uterine incision may lead to difficult fetal extraction.

409
00:51:39,148 --> 00:51:43,786
Thus, a vertical hysterotomy incision is typically indicated.

410
00:51:43,786 --> 00:51:52,561
With dorsoposterior or back up position, one or both feet can be grasped through a low transverse incision and delivered by breech extraction .

411
00:51:52,561 --> 00:52:00,773
Before labor or early in labor, with the membranes intact, attempts at external cephalic version (ECV) are worthwhile.

412
00:52:00,773 --> 00:52:09,398
Candidate selection and ECV technique mirror those for the breech fetus and are described in Chapter 28 (p. 528).

413
00:52:09,398 --> 00:52:18,398
ECV success rates are high and exceed those for breech fetuses (Correia Costa, 2020; Salzer, 2015).

414
00:52:18,398 --> 00:52:21,523
# Umbilical Cord Prolapse.

415
00:52:21,523 --> 00:52:30,811
Prolapse complicates 0.1 to 0.2 percent of births (Behbehani, 2016; Gibbons, 2014).

416
00:52:30,811 --> 00:52:36,736
As noted earlier, umbilical cord prolapse may be more common with pelvis contraction.

417
00:52:36,736 --> 00:52:49,936
Most risks stem from an unengaged presenting part and include hydramnios, breech presentation, transverse lie, premature or small fetus with weight <2500 g, preterm

418
00:52:50,092 --> 00:52:53,904
rupture of membranes, and multifetal gestation .

419
00:52:53,854 --> 00:52:58,854
Funic presentation is one in which the umbilical cord is the presenting part.

420
00:52:58,854 --> 00:53:05,429
Although rare, it is a potent risk factor for prolapse and merits cesarean delivery prior to labor.

421
00:53:05,429 --> 00:53:15,017
Of maternal factors for cord prolapse, grand multiparity, a distorting leiomyoma, or müllerian uterine anomaly are less common reasons .

422
00:53:15,017 --> 00:53:19,204
Umbilical cord prolapse is usually diagnosed clinically.

423
00:53:19,204 --> 00:53:25,267
The cord loop is palpated in a position lower in the vaginal canal than the head or beside it.

424
00:53:25,267 --> 00:53:31,192
For most cases, prompt manual elevation of the fetal head relieves cord compression.

425
00:53:31,192 --> 00:53:38,542
Concurrently, expeditious transfer to an operating room and preparations for cesarean delivery are completed.

426
00:53:38,542 --> 00:53:46,642
Rarely, vaginal or operative vaginal birth is reasonable if it can be completed much more rapidly than emergent cesarean birth .

427
00:53:46,642 --> 00:53:49,604
# Compound Presentation.

428
00:53:49,604 --> 00:53:57,017
With this, an extremity prolapses alongside the presenting part, and both present simultaneously in the pelvis .

429
00:53:57,017 --> 00:54:06,142
Goplerud and Eastman (1953) identified a hand or arm prolapsed alongside the head once in every 700 deliveries.

430
00:54:06,142 --> 00:54:14,354
Much less common was prolapse of one or both lower extremities alongside a cephalic presentation or a hand alongside a breech.

431
00:54:14,354 --> 00:54:25,692
At Parkland Hospital, compound presentations were identified in only 68 of more than 70,000 singleton fetuses—an incidence of approximately 1 in 1000.

432
00:54:25,692 --> 00:54:34,042
Compound presentations form in cases that prevent or delay occlusion of the pelvic inlet and mirror those for other malpresentations.

433
00:54:34,042 --> 00:54:36,442
Figure 23-8.

434
00:54:36,442 --> 00:54:38,817
Compound presentation.

435
00:54:38,817 --> 00:54:42,729
A. The left hand is lying in front of the vertex.

436
00:54:42,729 --> 00:54:49,717
With further labor, the hand and arm may retract from the birth canal, and the head may then descend normally.

437
00:54:49,717 --> 00:54:57,929
B. Photograph of a small 34-week fetus with a compound presentation that delivered uneventfully with the hand presenting first.

438
00:54:57,929 --> 00:55:00,954
Mild bruising resolved uneventfully.

439
00:55:00,954 --> 00:55:04,854
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Elizabeth Mosier.

440
00:55:04,854 --> 00:55:09,067
In most cases, the prolapsed part should be left alone.

441
00:55:09,067 --> 00:55:15,142
It typically does not impede labor and often retracts out of the way with descent of the presenting part.

442
00:55:15,142 --> 00:55:25,467
If it fails to retract and if it appears to prevent descent of the head, the prolapsed part can be pushed gently upward and the head simultaneously downward by fundal pressure.

443
00:55:25,467 --> 00:55:32,392
In cases with a co-presenting hand, the fetus may reflexively retract the hand if pinched by the provider.

444
00:55:32,392 --> 00:55:45,017
In general, rates of perinatal mortality and morbidity are increased, but these mainly stem from effects of associated preterm birth, prolapsed umbilical cord, and traumatic obstetrical procedures.

445
00:55:45,017 --> 00:55:53,592
Tebes and coworkers (1999) described a rare case of pressure-induced forearm ischemia and later surgical amputation.

446
00:55:53,592 --> 00:55:56,167
Complications With Dystocia.

447
00:55:56,167 --> 00:56:05,367
Dystocia, especially if labor is prolonged, is associated with a higher incidence of several common obstetrical and neonatal complications.

448
00:56:05,367 --> 00:56:15,992
Noted earlier, maternal infection, either intrapartum chorioamnionitis or postpartum endomyometritis, is more common with desultory and prolonged labors.

449
00:56:15,992 --> 00:56:21,317
Postpartum hemorrhage from atony is increased with prolonged and augmented labors.

450
00:56:21,317 --> 00:56:29,617
Uterine tears at the time of second-stage cesarean delivery also occur at greater incidence if the fetal head is impacted in the pelvis.

451
00:56:29,617 --> 00:56:32,342
Uterine rupture is another risk.

452
00:56:32,342 --> 00:56:42,579
Abnormal thinning of the lower uterine segment creates a serious danger during prolonged labor (Delafield, 2018; Ronel, 2012).

453
00:56:42,579 --> 00:56:51,367
As described in Chapter 21 (p. 410), the upper segment of the uterus contracts, retracts, and expels the fetus.

454
00:56:51,367 --> 00:57:00,879
In response, the softened lower uterine segment and cervix dilate and thereby form a greatly expanded, thinned-out tube through which the fetus can pass.

455
00:57:00,879 --> 00:57:05,529
The boundary between these segments is the physiological retraction ring.

456
00:57:05,529 --> 00:57:16,129
When disproportion is so pronounced that fetal descent arrests, the lower uterine segment becomes increasingly stretched, and the normal retraction ring is unusually marked.

457
00:57:16,129 --> 00:57:24,792
Seldom encountered today, the pathological retraction ring of Bandl is associated with exaggerated thinning of the lower uterine segment.

458
00:57:24,792 --> 00:57:28,217
The ring may be seen clearly as a sharp uterine

459
00:57:28,372 --> 00:57:32,634
indentation and signifies impending lower segment rupture.

460
00:57:32,584 --> 00:57:39,522
Fistula formation may result from dystocia, in which the presenting part is firmly wedged into the pelvis.

461
00:57:39,522 --> 00:57:45,322
Excessive pressure is exerted against tissues lying between the leading part and the pelvic wall.

462
00:57:45,322 --> 00:57:56,184
Because of impaired circulation, necrosis may result and become evident several days after delivery as vesicovaginal, vesicocervical, or rectovaginal fistulas .

463
00:57:56,184 --> 00:58:01,397
Most often, pressure necrosis follows a very prolonged second stage.

464
00:58:01,397 --> 00:58:05,622
These are rare today except in undeveloped medical systems.

465
00:58:05,622 --> 00:58:10,884
Lower-extremity nerve injury in the mother can follow prolonged second-stage labor.

466
00:58:10,884 --> 00:58:15,497
These are discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 644).

467
00:58:15,497 --> 00:58:22,622
Fortunately, deficits are mainly sensory, and most resolve within 6 months of delivery in most women.

468
00:58:22,622 --> 00:58:28,772
Similar to the mother, the incidence of peripartum fetal sepsis rises with longer labors.

469
00:58:28,772 --> 00:58:37,034
Caput succedaneum and molding develop commonly and may be impressive (Fig. 22-7, p. 423) .

470
00:58:37,034 --> 00:58:47,709
Mechanical trauma such as nerve injury, fractures, and cephalohematoma also are more frequent and are discussed further in Chapter 33 (p. 608).

